# PHASE 2: ENTERPRISE-GRADE APPLICATION SECURITY

Conduct a comprehensive security audit and implement appropriate production-grade security controls.

Follow the OWASP Top 10 and applicable OWASP Application Security Verification Standard principles.

Do not simply install security packages and assume the application is secure.

Inspect the actual implementation and enforce security at the correct architectural layers.

## 2.1 Authentication Security

Inspect and strengthen:

- User registration.
- User login.
- Password handling.
- Password hashing.
- Session management.
- Token generation.
- Token verification.
- Token expiration.
- Refresh token rotation, if applicable.
- Logout.
- Password reset.
- Email verification.
- Account recovery.
- Session invalidation.

Requirements:

- Use an established, secure password hashing algorithm such as Argon2id or an appropriately configured bcrypt implementation.
- Never store plaintext passwords.
- Never return password hashes in API responses.
- Use secure token generation.
- Set appropriate token expiration.
- Implement safe refresh token handling.
- Prevent token reuse where applicable.
- Secure cookie settings when cookies are used.
- Configure Secure, HttpOnly, and appropriate SameSite attributes for authentication cookies.
- Prevent authentication bypass.
- Avoid exposing authentication secrets in frontend code.

Preserve the existing authentication architecture where it is already secure and functional.

## 2.2 Authorization and Access Control

Implement or strengthen server-side authorization.

Verify permissions for:

- Customers.
- Admins.
- Staff.
- Super Admins.
- Other existing roles.

Requirements:

- Role-Based Access Control (RBAC), if applicable.
- Least-privilege access.
- Backend authorization on protected APIs.
- Resource ownership validation.
- Object-level authorization.
- Protection against IDOR vulnerabilities.
- Protection against privilege escalation.
- Restricted administrative operations.

Never rely only on hiding buttons or pages in the frontend.

A user must never gain access to another user's private information simply by changing an ID in an API request.

Do not weaken existing authorization rules to make features work.

## 2.3 API Security

Inspect every sensitive API endpoint.

Implement or improve:

- Request validation.
- Authentication middleware.
- Authorization middleware.
- Rate limiting.
- Request size limits.
- Safe error responses.
- Input sanitization where appropriate.
- Secure CORS configuration.
- HTTP security headers.
- Protection against injection attacks.
- Protection against mass assignment.
- Protection against unsafe query operators.
- Protection against malformed requests.

Do not expose:

- Database credentials.
- Secret API keys.
- Authentication tokens.
- Internal stack traces.
- Sensitive database fields.
- Private customer information.

Never return a success response if the underlying operation failed.

## 2.4 Input Validation and Injection Protection

