# PHASE 6: PREMIUM INFINITE SCROLL PRODUCT LOADING

This is a mandatory feature requirement.

Implement or improve the existing product listing experience so that products are progressively loaded in batches of exactly 20 items per successful page request, except when fewer than 20 remain.

The first request must return a maximum of 20 products.

The next request must return the next batch of 20 products.

Continue loading as the customer scrolls.

Do not request or return the entire product collection.

## 6.1 Expected User Experience

Initial page load:

- Load the first 20 products.
- Display the products as soon as their data is ready.
- Avoid blocking the entire page unnecessarily.

When the customer scrolls near the end of the currently loaded product list:

- Detect the loading threshold.
- Request the next 20 products.
- Show a premium skeleton loading state.
- Append the newly returned products.
- Preserve the existing products.
- Continue only when more products are available.

Repeat this process until no more products remain.

## 6.2 Intersection Observer

Prefer IntersectionObserver or the project's existing equivalent to detect when the user approaches the end of the product list.

Use a reasonable rootMargin to begin fetching before the customer reaches the absolute bottom.

Do not use continuous scroll event handlers that trigger excessive state updates or API requests.

Clean up observers when the component unmounts or the query changes.

## 6.3 Premium Skeleton Loading UI

Create a professional skeleton loading experience consistent with the existing product card design.

While the next batch is loading, show approximately 4-8 lightweight skeleton product cards, depending on the current grid layout.

Each skeleton should reflect the actual product card structure:

- Image placeholder.
- Product title placeholder.
- Price placeholder.
- Optional discount placeholder.
- Rating placeholder.
- Button placeholder if needed.

Use a subtle shimmer or pulse animation.

The skeleton must:

- Have dimensions close to the real product cards.
- Prevent unnecessary layout shifts.
- Match the existing theme.
- Work on desktop, tablet, and mobile.
- Respect reduced-motion preferences.
- Avoid heavy animation effects.

Do not show fake product information during loading.

## 6.4 Loading Performance

Aim for a responsive experience.

Target the next 20-product request to complete within approximately 1-2 seconds under the defined normal test environment and network conditions.

This is a performance target, not a guaranteed artificial delay.

Do not insert a fixed one- or two-second timeout merely to make the animation visible.

If the backend responds quickly, display the real products quickly.

If the request takes longer, keep the skeleton visible until the actual response arrives.

Use real performance measurements.

## 6.5 Infinite Scroll API Contract

Adapt the existing API architecture where possible.

A scalable response should conceptually contain:

- products: current batch of products.
- nextCursor: next pagination cursor.
- hasMore: whether more products are available.
- limit: actual page size.

The exact property names must follow existing project conventions unless a change is technically necessary.

The backend must enforce a maximum page size of 20 for this specific public product browsing flow.

Never trust a client-provided limit without applying a server-side maximum.

## 6.6 Prevent Duplicate Requests

Implement protections against:

- Multiple observer triggers.
- Repeated API calls.
- Duplicate products.
- Out-of-order responses.
- Race conditions.
- Infinite request loops.
- Repeated loading after hasMore becomes false.

Use a proper loading lock or the existing query library's request-state mechanism.

Append only valid, unique products.

## 6.7 Filter and Search Changes

When the user changes:

- Search query.
- Category.
- Brand.
- Price range.
- Sort order.
- Product status filter.
- Other listing filters.

The product listing must:

1. Reset the previous pagination cursor.
2. Reset the relevant loaded product state.
3. Cancel or ignore stale requests.
4. Request the first 20 matching products.
5. Start a new infinite-scroll sequence.

Do not append products from an old search result to a new filter result.

Keep the current filter and sorting behavior intact.

## 6.8 End of Results

When there are no more products:

- Stop further requests.
- Disconnect or disable the load trigger appropriately.
- Display a subtle end-of-results message if suitable.
- Do not display endless skeleton loaders.

If the current category or search has no matching products, display the existing empty state or a professionally designed empty state consistent with the current UI.

## 6.9 Product Grid Responsiveness

Preserve the existing product card design.

Do not redesign the product cards unnecessarily.

Ensure the grid works correctly across:

- Desktop.
- Laptop.
- Tablet.
- Mobile.

Maintain consistent spacing, image ratios, card heights, and loading placeholders.

